# Phase 122 -- `-T {term}` goes, and the command line is `+{command}` alone.
#
# FOUR OF tools/zargv.py's THIRTY INVOCATIONS, and they are exactly the four that
# spell the removed option letter.  The set is not written from memory: the check
# computes it as the letters the input's `switch (c)` accepted and the output's does
# not, over zargv.py's own list, and requires a row to move IF AND ONLY IF one of its
# words is an option spelling one of them.
#
#   -T xterm             started and drew 2,117 bytes      -> exit 1, Unknown option
#   -T no-such-term-9x   started and drew 2,117 bytes      -> exit 1, Unknown option
#   -T                   Argument missing after: "-T"      -> exit 1, Unknown option
#   -Txterm              Garbage after option argument     -> exit 1, Unknown option
#
# The last two are the interesting half: they were already errors, and they moved
# anyway, because the two messages they gave are ME_ARG_MISSING and ME_GARBAGE --
# enumerators whose last use was the `-T` argument block, removed here with the
# main_errors[] rows they index.  A row that was already `Unknown option argument`
# before -- the file arguments, `--`, `-e`, `-R` and the rest of phase 88's leftovers --
# must NOT move, and the check requires that of all twenty-six.
#
# NOTHING ELSE MOVES, and three things that could have are worth naming.  The 102
# screen cases, the 98 Ex-command rows and the four pty scenarios are recorded from a
# binary started with no `-T` at all, so the compiled default applies either way.  The
# nineteen terminal rows are asked with `+set term={name}` (phase 116), which
# reaches did_set_term() and not termcapinit(), and that path is untouched: `term-moved`
# is declared at phase 121 and carries forward, but this phase's recording of the table
# is the INPUT's byte for byte.  And the fallback that phase 121 repaired is deleted
# here rather than fixed again -- it was reachable only through `-T`, which is the
# whole reason phase 121 had to repair it and this one may remove it.
#
# TWO CHANGES THIS DELTA CANNOT NAME, both measured by the phase's own probes because
# no harness records them:
#   * report_term_error()'s message. It still runs -- the run-time refusal calls it
#     before it fails -- and it now says `'vt320' not known` where it said
#     `defaulting to 'xterm-256color'`, which after this phase would be a promise
#     nothing keeps. ztermcheck.py drops every line that carries no `term=`, so the
#     message appears in no recording at all; a pty probe on both binaries is the
#     evidence, with E522 and the terminal the editor is left at unchanged.
#   * the `requested` fold. It changes what the 256-colour test reads only for a name
#     spelled `builtin_...`, which nothing in the corpus ever types; the probe is
#     `+set term=builtin_xterm-256color` and `+set term=builtin_debug` on both
#     binaries, identical.
argv:-T argv:-T_xterm argv:-Txterm argv:-T_no-such-term-9x
